From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Give the rate control room to work, and let it spend where it needs to

A cast holding a constant rate on a link with little room was found to drop
nothing at all and still look wrong: the picture swung between sharp and soft
as what was on screen moved, worst wherever a window scrolled. No frame was
late, none was lost, and every one of them was the size it was asked to be.

That is the rate control doing what it was told with nowhere to do it. The
buffer it is allowed to borrow across was two frames, which is sixty-six
milliseconds at thirty a second, so a frame that costs more than its share
cannot take from its neighbours and is coarsened instead until it fits. The
same was found on the software encoder, where the advice reached was a
hundred to a hundred and thirty milliseconds against a default of six
hundred. Four frames is a hundred and thirty-three at thirty, and sixty-six
at sixty, which is where this began.

Within a frame, the same thing happens in miniature: the whole picture is
held to one quantiser, so a cost concentrated in one corner is paid by
coarsening all of it. Macroblock level rate control lets the bits go where
the picture needs them, and this encoder has it in every mode but the one
this is not.

Neither of these is reachable on this encoder by the route the software one
offers. Periodic intra refresh, which spreads the cost of recovery instead of
spending it all on one frame, exists only there; the VA low power entrypoint
does not expose it, and a shorter distance between keyframes is not a
substitute because it makes the spike more often rather than smaller.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
@@ -1135,6 +1135,15 @@
       if (vbv_kbit > 0)
         g_object_set (encoder, "cpb-size", vbv_kbit, NULL);
 
+      /* Let the rate control move bits about inside a frame rather than
+       * holding the whole of it to one quantiser. A frame whose cost is all
+       * in one corner otherwise has the entire picture coarsened to pay for
+       * it, which is seen as the whole screen going soft when one window
+       * scrolls. Not available in constant quantiser mode, which this is
+       * not. */
+      if (g_object_class_find_property (G_OBJECT_GET_CLASS (encoder), "mbbrc"))
+        g_object_set (encoder, "mbbrc", 1, NULL);
+
       g_message ("WfdMediaFactory: Encoding on the processor's own graphics.");
       break;
 
diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.h b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.h
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.h
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.h
@@ -176,7 +176,16 @@
 
   /* Started where the card was left, on the same link and the same receiver,
    * so that what differs between them is the encoder and not the numbers. */
-  [ELEMENT_VAH264LP]  = { .bitrate_kbit = 10 * 1024, .vbv_frames = 2 },
+  /* Four frames rather than two. The buffer is what the rate control is
+   * allowed to borrow across: with two frames at thirty a second it has
+   * sixty-six milliseconds, and a frame that is expensive to encode cannot
+   * borrow from its neighbours, so it is crushed instead and the picture
+   * swings between sharp and soft as the content moves. Measured advice on
+   * the software encoder, which has the same shape of control, was a hundred
+   * to a hundred and thirty milliseconds; four frames is a hundred and
+   * thirty-three at thirty and sixty-six at sixty, which is where this
+   * started. */
+  [ELEMENT_VAH264LP]  = { .bitrate_kbit = 10 * 1024, .vbv_frames = 4 },
 
   /* Never measured, and so given what a crowded band was measured carrying
    * rather than left at the receiver's own maximum.
